  "summary": "Neoclassical interiors inspired by ancient Rome and Napoleon's imperial ambitions.",
  "description": "Empire interiors feature bold geometric patterns, heavy mahogany furniture, gilded accents, and motifs like laurel wreaths, sphinxes, and eagles. This style developed under Napoleon I as a symbol of French imperial power, drawing directly from classical antiquity and Egyptian motifs following Napoleon's campaigns.",
  "culturalContext": "The Empire style (1804-1815) — Napoleon's state design under Percier and Fontaine — put imperial Rome and Egypt into furniture: mahogany-and-gilt-bronze beds as thrones, laurel wreaths, bees (Napoleon's emblem), swans and sphinxes, made by masters like Jacob-Desmalter. It claimed empire as furniture policy; exported across Europe (Biedermeier's parent, the American \"Empire\" being its cousin), the style's stiff grandeur still reads as \"imperial\" in period drama.",
